# Secure Document Sharing — High-Level Plan

## Goal

Improve HSI's existing document sharing feature so creators can safely share their screenplays and decks, control access, and understand how recipients engage with them.
Start with PDF documents. Add implementation details to each milestone when we are ready to work on it.

## First release

The first complete experience should be:

**Upload a PDF → share with an invited person → verify their email → view the original pages → track the visit → revoke access.**

The roadmap below covers that first release and later improvements. Later features can be prioritized after the core experience works.

## Roadmap

### Milestone 1 — Understand DocSend

**Status: research complete.** Reviewed DocSend's official product pages and help articles. This was documentation research, not a hands-on trial account walkthrough.

#### What DocSend does

DocSend lets someone upload a document and share a link to it. The owner can choose access settings, see who visits and how long they spend on each page, and turn off access afterward. [DocSend overview](https://www.docsend.com/how-it-works/).

Its basic workflow is:

**Upload → create a link → choose access settings → share → recipient opens it → owner checks engagement.**

The relevant features are:

- **Document uploads and viewing:** supports PDFs, PowerPoint, Word, and other formats. Our first release will focus on PDFs. [Supported uploads](https://help.dropbox.com/create-upload/dropbox-docsend-upload-files).
- **Access controls:** email requirements, email verification, allowed viewers, passwords, expiration, and download settings. Asking for an email and verifying ownership of that email are separate options. [Link settings](https://help.dropbox.com/share/dropbox-docsend-link-settings-in-content-library).
- **Verified recipients:** verification is tied to the device used to open the document. This matters when a link is forwarded. [Email authentication](https://help.dropbox.com/share/dropbox-docsend-email-authentication).
- **Personalized watermarks:** can show the viewer's email, date, time, and other details. These discourage unauthorized sharing. [Watermarks](https://help.dropbox.com/share/dropbox-docsend-watermarks).
- **Viewing statistics:** visits, page activity, completion, and document version performance. Time spent is an estimate of viewing, not proof of reading; an idle foreground tab can still count. [Analytics](https://www.docsend.com/features/analytics/), [Time tracking](https://help.dropbox.com/share/dropbox-docsend-visitor-time-on-document).
- **Updated documents:** replacing a file updates its existing links, and performance can be compared across versions. Recoverable earlier files are a separate requirement we should define for HSI. [Document updates](https://help.dropbox.com/view-edit/dropbox-docsend-update-existing-content).
- **Later features:** multiple documents under one link and agreements before viewing. Some advanced features depend on the DocSend plan. [Spaces and agreements](https://www.docsend.com/how-it-works/), [Link settings](https://help.dropbox.com/share/dropbox-docsend-link-settings-in-content-library).

#### HSI feature checklist

For the first release, HSI should let a creator:

- Upload a PDF deck or screenplay and preserve its original layout.
- Create separate sharing links for different recipients.
- Verify the recipient's email before opening restricted material.
- Set expiration, revoke access, and choose download permissions.
- Display a personalized watermark.
- See visits, actual pages viewed, and approximate viewing time.

Later, add reliable draft history, stronger feedback tools, and sharing multiple documents together.

**Done when:** we can explain the sharing experience we want to build in plain language.

**Result:** the workflow and feature checklist above are ready to use for the HSI comparison.

### Milestone 2 — Compare DocSend with HSI

**Status: code comparison complete; live walkthrough and scope decisions pending.**

Reviewed the existing HSI upload, sharing, viewing, analytics, and version code against the checklist. "Present" below means it exists in the code; it has not yet been confirmed working in a live test environment.

#### Comparison checklist

| Feature | HSI today | What we should do |
|---|---|---|
| PDF uploading | Present; the upload approach can hit size limits | Improve support for larger decks |
| Original PDF layout for external recipients | Needs improvement; shared links display extracted text | Preserve the original pages and images |
| Separate sharing links | Present | Reuse and test |
| Recipient access and email verification | Present, with identity-check problems | Fix first so someone cannot claim another person's access |
| Password, expiration, and link deactivation | Present | Test each setting and how it affects an existing viewer |
| Download permissions | Basic; the external viewer exports text | Define and enforce the intended file/download behavior |
| Personalized watermark | Basic; currently a visual overlay | Improve it for the agreed PDF viewing and download behavior |
| Visits and page statistics | Present; external page numbers come from reconstructed text | Track the real PDF pages and verified viewers |
| Visit notifications | Present | Check email delivery in the test environment |
| Notes and confidentiality agreements | Present | Review and improve later |
| Updates behind existing links | Present | Test that recipients receive the intended version |
| Recoverable earlier PDF versions | Incomplete | Preserve earlier files in Milestone 8 |
| Multiple documents under one external link | Not found in the current external sharing flow | Consider in Milestone 10 |

The supporting code references and findings are in [secure-document-sharing-review.md](C:/Users/Yun/OneDrive/Documents/bdap/research/secure-document-sharing-review.md).

#### Prioritized changes

1. **Fix access security — Milestone 3.** Make sure only authorized people can open documents and that verification belongs to the actual viewer.
2. **Improve PDF uploading and viewing — Milestone 4.** Handle larger decks and preserve their layout.
3. **Finish sharing controls — Milestone 5.** Make verification, expiry, revocation, downloads, and watermarks work consistently together.
4. **Improve analytics — Milestone 6.** Report the actual PDF pages, verified recipient, and approximate viewing time.
5. **Test the complete experience — Milestone 7.** Confirm the core flow before releasing it.

#### Proposed first-release scope

Start with one PDF shared through a link with an external recipient. Include email verification, personalized watermarks, expiration, revocation, download settings, and basic page analytics. Advanced features remain in later milestones.

We still need to settle:

- The maximum PDF size and expected document length.
- Who owns changes to sharing, login, and storage.
- Whether controlled access is sufficient, or whether HSI's servers must also be unable to read the document. This affects viewing and AI analysis.

#### Live walkthrough still needed

A complete walkthrough needs a confirmed test version of HSI with sample documents and test recipients. The local project has installed dependencies and a built frontend, but its database, storage, and email connections have not been confirmed as test services. Its local and deployed access paths also differ.

Once the test environment is available, check:

1. Upload and share a sample PDF deck.
2. Open it as the intended recipient and verify their email.
3. Check layout and viewing statistics.
4. Try the link as a different recipient.
5. Expire or revoke the link and check that future access is denied.

**Done when:** we have a prioritized list of changes and know who owns each part.

**Result so far:** the comparison and priorities are ready. Live behavior, remaining scope, and team ownership are still to be confirmed.

### Milestone 3 — Fix Access Security

- Make sure private documents are accessible only to authorized people.
- Fix the identity-check problems found in the code review.
- Make sure entering someone else's email or receiving a forwarded link does not grant their access.

**Done when:** the intended recipient can gain access, while someone claiming their identity cannot.

This is the first implementation milestone.

### Milestone 4 — Improve PDF Uploading and Viewing

- Support decks up to the agreed file size.
- Preserve the original pages, images, and layout.
- Give recipients a clear reading experience on desktop and mobile.

**Done when:** a creator can upload a PDF deck and the recipient can read it with its original layout intact.

### Milestone 5 — Complete Sharing Controls

- Let creators choose who can view a document.
- Complete email verification, expiration, and access revocation.
- Define and improve download permissions and personalized watermarks.
- Review existing passcode options and keep the settings easy to understand.

**Done when:** the creator's chosen settings consistently control future access to the document.

### Milestone 6 — Improve Viewing Analytics

- Show who opened a document and when.
- Track actual document pages and approximate engagement time.
- Present visits and page activity in a simple owner dashboard.

**Done when:** creators can understand a recipient's engagement with the real document, with timing clearly treated as an estimate.

### Milestone 7 — Test and Release the Core Experience

- Test the complete upload, share, verify, view, track, and revoke flow.
- Check unauthorized access, forwarded links, expired links, and revoked links.
- Check larger files, common devices, and clear error messages.
- Confirm that the released application behaves like the tested version.

**Done when:** the first-release experience works reliably in the agreed release environment.

Check each milestone as it is built; this milestone brings the full experience together.

### Milestone 8 — Add Reliable Document Version History

- Preserve earlier drafts and decks when a creator uploads an update.
- Decide whether a shared link follows the newest version or stays on a selected version.
- Show which version a recipient viewed.

**Done when:** creators can recover earlier versions and understand which material was shared and read.

### Milestone 9 — Improve Feedback and Follow-Up

- Review notifications when someone opens a document.
- Improve recipient feedback and the creator's private notes.
- Consider confidentiality agreements for sensitive documents.
- Later, explore AI-assisted engagement summaries and follow-up drafts, with clear rules for using document content and creator review before sending.

**Done when:** creators can act on viewing activity through the features we choose to prioritize.

### Milestone 10 — Expand Based on Real Use

- Consider additional document formats, such as native presentation files.
- Consider sharing multiple documents together.
- Consider team permissions and collaboration needs.
- Prioritize improvements based on feedback from creators and recipients.

**Done when:** each selected expansion solves a demonstrated user need.

